Trame \cite{trame} is a Python framework that makes it possible to create
reactive web applications with no web development knowledge needed. Trame and
trame-slicer's ecosystem, architecture, and use cases will be described in
\cref{sec:context}.

The internship has two main objectives.

One is to discover all the development of this framework which has around 15
part time developers. This includes the documentation, the maintenance, the
design choices, the ecosystem, the communication, and the support. These points
will be discussed throughout this report.

The second objective is to take part in this development by actively
contributing, acquiring skills on all the tools, modules, practices that are
used in \citev{trame}, trame-slicer and their ecosystem's development.  These
are about Continuous Integration (CI) and Continuous Deployment (CD), the use
of new languages, the User Experience (UX) and User Interface (UI), the agile
methodology, Test Driven Development (TDD), the design patterns of huge
softwares.

Different tasks have been identified and performed during this project and fit
in the following categories

\begin{itemize} 

\item Feedback on the trame resources 

\item Maintenance work 

\item Develop a trame widget for an easy tool selection (Radial Menu) 

\item Make trame-slicer mobile/tablet friendly 

\end{itemize}

All the tasks that were achieved fit in \cref{fig:timeline} where "Radial Menu"
(resp. "Trame Slicer") contain all the working days where a meeting concerning
it or a commit in the \citev{trame-radial-menu} (resp.\refrepo{trame-slicer})
repo was made, "Tech" stands for all the tech presentations, and "Point" stands
for all the working days there was a follow up meeting concerning the
